Manage URL lists
.SH SYNOPSIS

[1] \fBurllist\fR [\fBraz\fR | (\fBadd\fR | \fBdel\fR) \fI<urllist-name>\fR | \fBupdate\fR | \fBreport\fR]

[2] \fBurllist\fR [(\fBload\fR | \fBvload\fR) [(\fBcreate\fR | \fBupdate\fR | \fBauto\fR) \fI<urllist-name>\fR (\fBftp\fR | \fBsftp\fR | \fBtftp\fR) \fI<file-server>\fR \fI<base-file-name>\fR [(\fBdomains\fR | \fBurls\fR | \fBexpressions\fR)*]]]

[3] \fBurllist\fR [\fBclear\fR \fI<urllist-name>\fR [(\fBdomains\fR | \fBurls\fR | \fBexpressions\fR)*]]

[4] \fBurllist auto\fR [\fI<urllist-name>\fR [\fBoff\fR | (\fBon\fR (\fBload\fR | \fBvload\fR) (\fBcreate\fR | \fBupdate\fR) (\fBdaily\fR | \fBweekly\fR) (\fBpush\fR | (\fBftp\fR | \fBsftp\fR | \fBtftp\fR) \fI<file-server>\fR \fI<base-file-name>\fR))]]

.SH DESCRIPTION
This system allows you to filter the access to URLs and domain names according to policies and rules (see the \fBguard\fR command). The command \fBurllist\fR allows you to manage those lists of URLs and domain names. A URL list is represented by a unique name in the system. First, an empty URL list must be created. The first [1] usage form allows you to create, delete or update URL lists. To create an empty URL list use the keyword \fBadd\fR followed by a URL list name. A URL list name must begin with an alpha character and may contains alpha numeric characters as well as the characters "_" and "-". To delete a URL list use the keyword \fBdel\fR followed by the URL list name to delete. The keyword \fBraz\fR allows you to delete all URL lists.

The system can be configured to periodically update URL list contents (see later). In the first usage form the keyword \fBupdate\fR allows you to explicitly update URL list contents without having to wait for the periodic automatic updates. This could be useful for instance with a suspended virtual machine having a non updated system time (in this case do not forgetgorget to update the system time prior to updating URL list contents). Using the keyword \fBreport\fR allows you to display the latest report of the automatic URL list content updating. During such an update if a URL list can't be loaded, an error is occurred. For error explanations see the section \fBDOWNLOAD ERROR CODES\fR below.

The second [2] usage form allows you to load URL lists from files located on a file server. Only trusted file servers are allowed. Trusted file servers are defined with the command \fBaccess\fR. To load and build a URL list from scratch use the keyword \fBload\fR followed by the keyword \fBcreate\fR. The content of a URL list to load should be defined in three compressed (gzip format) text files having the same base name and three different extensions. The first file has the \fI.domains.gz\fR extension and contains a list of domain base names (one domain base name per line). Only domain \fBbase\fR name should be specified (for instance "example.com" is a valid domain base name while "www.example.com" is not). The second file has the extension \fI.urls.gz\fR and contains a list of URLs (one URL per line). A URL is in the form \fI<domain-name>/<loaction>\fR. Please note that the protocol part (for instance "http://") of the URL should not be specified in that file. Finally the third file has the extension \fI.expressions.gz\fR and contains a list of regular expressions (one regular expression per line). See the section \fBREGULAR EXPRESSIONS\fR below.

When defining a domain name, do not specify the prefixes www, web and ftp. For a URL definition, protocol parts (http://, ftp://...), prefixes (www, web, ftp) and ports (:80, :443...) should not be specified. An expression is a regular expression as described in regex (see the regex (5) manual on a UNIX system). Note that complex regular expressions require large CPU resources.

A \fBdomain\fR or \fBurl\fR diff file contains lines beginning with "-" (minus) or "+" (plus). To add a domain or a URL precede it with the character "-". To delete a domain or a URL precede it with the character "+". The \fI<base-file-name>\fR parameter is the base file name (without extensions \fI.domains.gz\fR, \fI .urls.gz\fR or \fI.expressions.gz\fR) of a defined URL list. File(s) must exist and be accessible on the file server. The optional last arguments specify which URL list types to load. URL list types are: \fBdomains\fR, \fBurls\fR and \fBexpressions\fR. If no URL list types are specified, \fBdomains\fR and \fBurls\fR URL list types are loaded.

To update an existing URL list use the keyword \fBupdate\fR. In this case downloaded files are diff files. Only \fBdomains\fR and \fBurls\fR files can be updated. If more than one update file is loaded for the same URL list, updates well be successively applied to that URL list. In this case the order in which update files are loaded is important.

To automatically download all updates since the last create or update operation, use the keyword \fBauto\fR. In this case downloaded files are diff files and should be named as follows: \fI<base-file-name>.<yyyymmdd>.(domains | urls).gz\fR where \fI<yyyymmdd>\fR is the date (\fIyyyy\fR is the year, \fImm\fR is the month and \fIdd\fR is the day). In the case where the URL list has never been loaded before and the update mode is used, the create mode is used (the URL list is entirely loaded from scratch).

The \fBvload\fR (verify load) option allows you to secure downloads. This is useful when you download URL lists from a file server managed by CacheGuard Technologies or one of its referenced partners. When using the \fBvload\fR method, a signature file is downloaded alongside the URL list file and the URL list file is verified using that signature file to assure that the downloaded URL list file has not been altered during its transfer. The signature file name has the same name as the downloaded URL list file followed by the extension \fI.sig\fR. You can print a list of all downloaded files by using the keyword \fBload\fR (or \fBvload\fR) without any other options.

Please note a URL list file in gzip compressed format can't be larger than than 128 MB. In case where \fBdomains\fR, \fBurls\fR and \fBexpressions\fR files are all loaded (separately or at the same time), if one of them exceeds the allowed limit, all of them are deleted and the current load operation is immediately cancelled. To load files larger than the allowed limit, you have the possibility to split them into smaller pieces.

The third [3] usage form allows you clear the content of a URL list without completely deleting that list. You can select what parts of the URL list should be cleared by specifying the \fBdomains\fR, \fBurls\fR \fBexpressions\fR keywords. If no part name is specified, all parts are cleared.

The fourth [4] usage form allows you to program the system to automatically download and apply URL list contents. This can be done \fBdaily\fR or \fBweekly\fR. To activate the automatic updating for a URL list use the \fBauto on\fR keywords followed by download specifications. To download the complete URL list use the \fBcreate\fR keyword. In case where a complete URL list is downloaded, files to download should be named as follows: \fI<base-file-name>.(domains | urls).gz\fR. Please note that both domains and urls files should exist on the remote file server. To only download updates (diff files) use the \fBupdate\fR keyword. Because downloading and rebuilding a complete URL list can be quite time and bandwidth consuming, always prefer the \fBupdate\fR mode rather than the \fBcreate\fR mode. The \fBupdate\fR mode allows the periodical download of all updates since the last \fBcreate\fR or \fBupdate\fR operation. This way, in case of an unavailability of a file server, not update files are lost. To allow the system to automatically download all update files since the last automatic update, files should be named as follows: \fI<base-file-name>.<yyyymmdd>.(domains | urls).gz\fR where \fI<yyyymmdd>\fR is the date (\fIyyyy\fR is the year, \fImm\fR is the month and \fIdd\fR is the day).

Automatic downloads can be done using the \fBftp\fR, \fBsftp\fR or \fBtftp\fR protocols. In case where the system is managed by a remote \fImanager\fR system, the manager can be configured to download URL lists and push them to all gateways that it manages. In this case, you must specify the \fBpush\fR method as the used protocol. The \fBAUTO UPDATES ON A MANAGER SYSTEM\fR section below gives more information regarding automatic URL lists updates on a \fImanager\fR system.

To deactivate the automatic updating for a URL list use the keywords \fBauto\fR followed by the keyword \fBoff\fR.

